    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper describes the concepts behind a computer code for quantitatively analyzing outcrop fracture data. Fracture total length, area spacing, spatial density, and other weighted parameters are calculated on a grid basis. The effects of boundary truncation on the statistics of the fracture trace length distribution are evaluated. If the fracture average length is of the same order as outcrop size, the mapped fracture traces will not reflect the true fracture trace length statistics. A general methodology for fracture prediction in a reservoir beyond the well bore is described, and ways of improving prediction accuracy are discussed.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
